From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] cpuidle: psci: keep DT idle states unusable when the firmware refuses CPU_SUSPEND

What. psci_init_cpu_suspend() already asks the firmware
PSCI_FEATURES(CPU_SUSPEND) and, on NOT_SUPPORTED, only leaves the format
flags at zero -- CPU_SUSPEND stays wired and cpuidle-psci registers every DT
idle state as usable. Record the refusal in psci.c, export it as
psci_cpu_suspend_supported(), and have cpuidle-psci mark every PSCI state
CPUIDLE_FLAG_UNUSABLE when it is false. WFI stays state 0, the driver still
registers, the states stay visible in sysfs with usage and rejected at 0.

Why. On our chain (open TF-A v2.12.0, f4c0b0dba) CPU_SUSPEND is not offered:
plat_setup_psci_ops() falls back to sunxi_native_psci_ops, which has no
pwr_domain_suspend and no validate_power_state, so lib/psci/psci_setup.c:
251-254 never sets the CPU_SUSPEND cap. The firmware is honest about it --
psci_features() returns NOT_SUPPORTED for a missing cap
(lib/psci/psci_main.c:379-381) -- and every CPU_SUSPEND is answered with
SMC_UNK (psci_main.c:446-447), -EOPNOTSUPP in Linux (measured 2026-08-15). The kernel is what
ignores the answer, in three places of 6.18.36:

  - psci.c:664 wires psci_ops.cpu_suspend unconditionally, and
    psci_init_cpu_suspend() (psci.c:585-591) does not unset it. For
    SYSTEM_SUSPEND the same file does gate on the answer (psci.c:581) --
    the asymmetry is the defect.
  - a rejected entry returns -1; cpuidle_enter_state() counts it in
    `rejected` (cpuidle.c:334-336) and returns without WFI. do_idle()
    loops (kernel/sched/idle.c:298) and asks the governor again.
  - menu learns nothing from it: cpuidle_reflect() drops index < 0
    (cpuidle.c:405), and menu_select() records the rejection as an
    interval of UINT_MAX (menu.c:230-238), which pushes the next
    prediction long, i.e. back to the deepest state. No kernel code turns
    a state off after repeated rejections.

So an idle core spins through cpu_pm_enter(), tick_broadcast_enter() (the
states carry local-timer-stop), the arm64 cpu_suspend() context save and one
SMC to EL3 and back, over and over, until an interrupt ends the idle period.
Measured on an idle board (load 0.03, 8047 s uptime):
WFI `time` covers only
2973 s on cpu0, 3620 s on cpu2 and 5234 s on cpu6; the rest of the wall
clock, 35-63 %, is in no idle state at all, against 1.0-1.7e8 rejections
per CPU -- roughly 30 us per rejected round trip. Deep idle was lost when we
left the vendor BL31; this spin is what replaced it, and it is worse than
the WFI it is meant to beat.

Why here and not in the DT. The DT describes the states correctly: the vendor
BL31 v2.5 entered both with these exact parameters on this board (boot of 2026-07-21), and the vendor's own DT carries them
byte-identical. What changed is the firmware, and the firmware says so at
boot. Dropping cpu-idle-states from `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` would (1) take the DT out of step
with the hardware and put it back in step only by a second DT edit once the
firmware learns CPU_SUSPEND, and (2) stop cpuidle-psci from probing at all
(dt_init_idle_driver() returns 0 -> -ENODEV), which also removes the
cpuidle_cooling_register() call at cpuidle-psci.c:412 -- the only thing that
registers `arm64-dts-a733-idle-injection-cooling`'s six idle-cpuN cooling devices, proven on hardware. Unsetting
psci_ops.cpu_suspend in psci.c would do the same via cpuidle-psci.c:324.
Marking the states unusable keeps both the driver and the cooling devices.

Why UNUSABLE and not OFF. CPUIDLE_FLAG_OFF sets the user bit, and a write
of 0 to .../disable would put the spin back. UNUSABLE sets the driver bit
(cpuidle.c:650-651): the firmware said no, sysfs cannot overrule it.
default_status reads "enabled" and disable reads 0 for these states; what
tells the reader is the boot log (two lines below) and rejected = 0.

No vendor code. Nothing A733-specific either: any PSCI >= 1.0 firmware that
reports NOT_SUPPORTED gets the same treatment, and firmware before 1.0 has no
PSCI_FEATURES, so it is assumed to support CPU_SUSPEND as before.

Expected on silicon, open TF-A:
  psci: CPU_SUSPEND not supported by firmware
  CPUidle PSCI: firmware refuses CPU_SUSPEND, 1 DT idle state(s) unusable
  /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpuidle/state1/{usage,rejected} = 0
(The count is drv->state_count - 1, read at run time. It printed 2 while
`arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` declared cluster-sleep-0 as well; `arm64-dts-a733-psci-idle-states` v2 declares only
cpu-sleep-0.)
  and WFI `time` close to wall-clock time on an idle board;
  /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/type still lists idle-cpu2..idle-cpu7.
Falsifier: rejected climbing on state1 with this patch in; or the first
line missing while /sys/kernel/debug/psci prints
"PSCI_FEATURES(CPU_SUSPEND) error (-1)" (then the probe path is wrong); or
idle-cpuN cooling devices gone. Under a BL31 that offers CPU_SUSPEND (the
vendor v2.5) neither line may appear and the states must be entered as they were
under the vendor BL31 on 2026-07-21 -- anything else means this patch hides a working state.

How to get deep idle back once the firmware limitation is fixed: nothing to revert. A TF-A that
implements pwr_domain_suspend + validate_power_state sets the cap,
PSCI_FEATURES answers with the format flags, psci_cpu_suspend_supported()
returns true and the states are usable with the DT unchanged. The two log
lines disappearing is the signal. This patch can stay; it only acts on a
firmware that refuses.

For a comparison build without it: A7S_SKIP_PATCHES=cpuidle-psci-keep-states-unusable-when-firmware-refuses-cpu-suspend

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
+++ b/drivers/firmware/psci/psci.c
@@ -77,6 +77,7 @@
 				PSCI_1_0_EXT_POWER_STATE_TYPE_MASK)
 
 static u32 psci_cpu_suspend_feature;
+static bool psci_cpu_suspend_refused;
 static bool psci_system_reset2_supported;
 static bool psci_system_off2_hibernate_supported;
 
@@ -91,6 +92,16 @@
 	return psci_cpu_suspend_feature & PSCI_1_0_OS_INITIATED;
 }
 
+/*
+ * False only when the firmware has said so: PSCI_FEATURES(CPU_SUSPEND)
+ * answered NOT_SUPPORTED. Before PSCI 1.0 there is no PSCI_FEATURES to ask,
+ * and CPU_SUSPEND is assumed, as it always has been.
+ */
+bool psci_cpu_suspend_supported(void)
+{
+	return !psci_cpu_suspend_refused;
+}
+
 static inline bool psci_power_state_loses_context(u32 state)
 {
 	const u32 mask = psci_has_ext_power_state() ?
@@ -586,8 +597,12 @@
 {
 	int feature = psci_features(PSCI_FN_NATIVE(0_2, CPU_SUSPEND));
 
-	if (feature != PSCI_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED)
+	if (feature != PSCI_RET_NOT_SUPPORTED) {
 		psci_cpu_suspend_feature = feature;
+	} else {
+		psci_cpu_suspend_refused = true;
+		pr_info("CPU_SUSPEND not supported by firmware\n");
+	}
 }
 
 /*
--- a/include/linux/psci.h
+++ b/include/linux/psci.h
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 bool psci_power_state_is_valid(u32 state);
 int psci_set_osi_mode(bool enable);
 bool psci_has_osi_support(void);
+bool psci_cpu_suspend_supported(void);
 
 struct psci_operations {
 	u32 (*get_version)(void);
--- a/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
+++ b/drivers/cpuidle/cpuidle-psci.c
@@ -409,6 +409,23 @@
 		return ret;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * The DT describes the states, the firmware decides whether they can be
+	 * entered. If it answered PSCI_FEATURES(CPU_SUSPEND) with NOT_SUPPORTED,
+	 * every entry would be one SMC round trip that returns an error, and the
+	 * idle loop would re-select the same state at once: nothing in cpuidle
+	 * learns from a rejection. Keep the driver -- WFI and the cooling device
+	 * registered below still need it -- and make the PSCI states unusable.
+	 */
+	if (!psci_cpu_suspend_supported()) {
+		int i;
+
+		for (i = 1; i < drv->state_count; i++)
+			drv->states[i].flags |= CPUIDLE_FLAG_UNUSABLE;
+		pr_warn_once("firmware refuses CPU_SUSPEND, %d DT idle state(s) unusable\n",
+			     drv->state_count - 1);
+	}
+
 	ret = cpuidle_register(drv, NULL);
 	if (ret)
 		goto deinit;
